WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.580
Administration scripts need explicit error handling because operational environments are messy.

2
00:00:06.680 --> 00:00:13.400
Hosts are offline, permissions differ, services are busy, files are locked, and data formats change.

3
00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:17.580
PowerShell has both terminating and non-terminating errors.

4
00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:24.240
At a high level, some failures stop execution, while others report a problem and allow the pipeline to continue.

5
00:00:24.340 --> 00:00:28.500
Scripts should intentionally decide which behavior is appropriate for each operation.

6
00:00:28.780 --> 00:00:34.140
Try, catch, and finally blocks help separate normal logic from failure handling and cleanup.

7
00:00:34.240 --> 00:00:38.480
ErrorAction can help turn certain failures into behavior that the script can catch.

8
00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:45.060
Exit codes are useful when scripts are run by scheduled tasks, automation platforms, or monitoring tools.

9
00:00:45.160 --> 00:00:50.680
The goal is to make failures observable and actionable, not to suppress them so the script appears successful.

10
00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:54.320
Logging should be structured and sensitive to context.

11
00:00:54.420 --> 00:01:04.900
Transcripts and logs can capture commands, paths, usernames, hostnames, output, errors, and sometimes data that should not be broadly shared.

12
00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:11.480
Scripts should log enough to support audit and troubleshooting while avoiding unnecessary secrets, file contents, or protected data.

13
00:01:11.580 --> 00:01:16.940
Logs should also have an owner, location, retention expectation, and access model.

14
00:01:17.220 --> 00:01:21.060
Testing raises confidence before automation touches production.

15
00:01:21.160 --> 00:01:26.920
PSScriptAnalyzer can support static review for style, quality, and common issues.

16
00:01:27.020 --> 00:01:32.060
Pester can test expected behavior, parameter handling, and helper functions.

17
00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:39.920
Dry runs, sample target lists, staged rollout, peer review, and change windows all help catch mistakes.

18
00:01:40.020 --> 00:01:43.060
A production-ready script is not only a clever command.

19
00:01:43.160 --> 00:01:48.840
It is an operated artifact that has been reviewed, tested, logged, and rolled out carefully.
